#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Forward a client's settled BTC deposit into the ThorChain swap.

STAGES 2 AND 3 of the BTC-intake rework (STAGE2_PLAN.md, STAGE3_PLAN.md).
This is the vault-side tool the `forward_to_swap` job runs. It takes ONE
deposit address the Pi has been watching, every settled output on it, and
builds and signs the transaction that pays ThorChain's current inbound
vault with the swap memo in an OP_RETURN. In DRY RUN it prints the result
and writes it to a 0600 file and sends nothing; with --broadcast it hands
the bytes it just signed to the network over Tor, from memory, and records
what the network said. Exactly one of the two flags is required: neither
is a refusal, so no mode is ever reached by omission.

The broadcast lives in gs_btc_broadcast, a module the Pi never imports;
the watch client this tool looks with still knows no method that could
move money. The signed bytes are not read from a file: what is sent is
what was signed a moment ago, in this process (STAGE3_PLAN.md 3.2) -- with
ONE exception, stage 5's --reconcile, which re-sends the bytes this tool
itself kept in its own plan because the network had not shown them.

STAGE 5 (STAGE5_PLAN.md): --reconcile, the third mode. A forward already
went out and the plan says so; the run asks the network what became of it
and acts: confirms it is listed (and brings the plan up to date), re-sends
kept bytes, re-signs an evicted transaction, forwards money that came
back to the address (a refund, a second payment) leaving out every output
a listed forward of ours consumed, or FAILS on a spend this tool did not
sign. A fresh forward rotates the earlier plan aside rather than
overwriting it: the plan chain is the record of every forward the deposit
had. And on any run, an address that holds nothing unspent is read for its
last spend before "not yet" is answered (reconcile_emptied), and a
refusal about today's fee is a word (delayed / short), not a dead end.

What it does, in order, and what it refuses at each step:

  1. Derives the deposit address from the account xpub and the index -- the
     same public derivation the Pi does -- and asks an Electrum server over
     Tor what settled money sits there, plus a fee estimate, in ONE session
     on the address's own circuit. Refuses when nothing is settled.
  2. Sizes the fee against the LARGEST transaction the policy allows (the
     memo is not known yet: the quote needs the amount, the amount needs
     the fee, the fee needs the memo). The real transaction is never larger,
     so the real fee-rate is never under the target. Refuses a fee estimate
     the server does not have, or one outside the operator's band.
  3. Asks SwapKit for a quote for EXACTLY the amount that will be sent, to
     the XMR subaddress this deposit was minted for. The quote returns the
     inbound vault address, the memo already bound to that destination, and
     the expected Monero. Refuses a memo that does not name our destination,
     a memo that will not fit the OP_RETURN policy, an inbound address that
     is not this network's, a quote whose worst-case arrival is under the
     mix floor, and a quote that deviates from the price oracle.
  4. Optionally cross-checks the inbound address against THORNode's own
     inbound_addresses over a second circuit, and refuses if the chain is
     halted or the addresses differ: a compromised aggregator naming its
     own address is the failure this catches.
  5. Builds the transaction (inputs = the settled outputs, out0 = inbound,
     out1 = OP_RETURN; no change -- the whole deposit is forwarded and the
     sizing slack goes to the miner rather than to a change output that
     would chain forwards together on-chain).
  6. Takes the seed from the ENVIRONMENT (GS_BTC_SEED, a BIP39 mnemonic --
     never argv, never a file this tool reads), proves it derives the very
     account xpub the Pi watches, proves the key it derives IS the deposit
     address, refuses to sign unless the constant-time system libsecp256k1
     is the live backend, signs, verifies every signature, measures the
     real size and rate.
  7. With --broadcast, and only then: refuses a quote older than
     --quote-max-age (nothing is sent on a stale price), hands the signed
     bytes to the configured Electrum servers in turn over Tor
     (gs_btc_broadcast.submit), and then asks the deposit address's history
     until the transaction is listed or --seen-wait runs out
     (gs_btc_broadcast.seen). The outcome is one of four words --
     accepted, rejected, ambiguous, unreachable -- and the word decides
     everything after it (STAGE3_PLAN.md 3.10): rejected by every server
     is a refusal (nothing moved); unreachable is a failure (nothing
     moved); accepted and ambiguous exit 0 because the money may have
     moved, and rendering that as "failed" is the false sentence the
     phase vocabulary exists to stop.
  8. Writes the plan -- inputs, outputs, memo, fee, rate, txid, the
     broadcast's outcome -- to the outfile, 0600, and prints a summary.
     The signed hex is in the plan ONLY when nothing consumes it yet and
     the network has not shown it: an unsigned plan (--plan-only), an
     explicit --write-signed-hex, or a broadcast whose transaction no
     server has listed (ambiguous, or accepted but unseen). A dry run
     prints the hex to stdout (the job log) and keeps it nowhere. The hex
     and the memo reach the job log and the file, never the hash chain,
     never a reply to the phone.

THE MEMO DOES NOT FIT A STANDARD OP_RETURN, and this tool says so rather
than hoping. A Monero subaddress is 95 characters; the shortest swap memo
is 105 bytes; the standard relay limit is 80. --op-return-max-bytes is the
operator's relay policy (a node run with a raised datacarrier limit can
carry more) and defaults to the standard 80, so on a default configuration
every real forward is REFUSED at step 3 with the byte count. The relay
strategy (STAGE3_PLAN.md 3.3) is failover: a server whose node keeps the
old policy rejects the memo, the next one relays it, and only every server
saying no is a refusal -- with their codes, and nothing moved.

What never reaches the hash chain: an address, an amount, a fee, a rate, a
txid, a memo, an index, a token, a server. Event kinds only, and the
per-output loop logs nothing at all. What never reaches argv: the seed,
the destination, the amount. What never reaches this file's output: the
seed.
"""
